 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT relating to legalizing, validating, ratifying and confirming a\ntransportation contract of the Fulton city school district",
    "basePrintNo": "S7775",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7775-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Transportation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7775\n\nSPONSOR: RITCHIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act relating to legalizing, validating, ratifying and confirming a\ntransportation contract of the Fulton city school district\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would correct a clerical error between the Fulton City School\nDistrict and the State Education Department in the 2016-2017 school year\nin effort to restore Transportation aid to the Fulton City School\nDistrict.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 legalizes, validates, ratifies, and confirms the transporta-\ntion contract of E006115 of the 2016-2017 school year.\n\nSection 2 directs the Department of Education to consider the contract\nfor transportation aid as valid and proper.\n\nSection 3 provides an immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAt its November 14, 2014 meeting, the Fulton City School District Board\nof Education extended its contract with its transportation service\nprovider for the 2016-2017 school year. Due to a clerical error, the\ncontract extension was not sent to the State Education Department.\n\nSchool officials were unaware that the contract extension had not been\nfiled with the state until the fall of 2017 when they were checking on\nthe status of their transportation aid. No notice had been provided to\nthe district that they had not filed. Upon learning of the error, the\nschool district reached out to State Education Department officials and\nwere informed the State Education Department does not have the discre-\ntionary authority to approve and pay transportation aid in these circum-\nstances - even if the department believes that the school should be in\nreceipt of those funds.\n\nThe loss of $1.6 million in transportation aid is very concerning and\nwill have significant impact on the Fulton City School District.  Those\ngreatest penalized for this unintentional error will be the students\nunless this special legislation is enacted. The impact on the school\ndistrict is especially harsh given the combined wealth ratio in the\ndistrict, which is among the worst in the state. When the district's\nwealth is measured taking into account real property values and income\nof its residents, it is obvious that the district is economically disad-\nvantaged making this legislation critical so that the education of the\nstudents of the Fulton City School District does not suffer.\n\nThis legislation will correct this inadvertent error and will grant the\nState Education Department the necessary authority to consider this\ncontract for transportation aid as valid and proper and therefore eligi-\nble for Transportation Funding.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nS.8730/A.10752.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to state; however, failure of this legislation will have a devas-\ntating fiscal impact on the Fulton City School District and its taxpay-\ners.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
